# Guide for adding or extending kernel experiments

The active H200 matrix is defined by [BENCHMARK_CONTRACT.md](BENCHMARK_CONTRACT.md)
version 3: 25 final workloads, with KDA intra-chunk and its 512-config pool.
That contract supersedes older case counts and KDA chunk-output descriptions
in this guide. Historical results retain their original contracts.

Follow the organization in [gemm/](gemm/README.md) and
[flash_attention/](flash_attention/README.md). Read their `cases.py`, `spaces.py`,
`kernel.py`, `reference.py`, and command entry points before adding a family.
The [experiment README](README.md) describes the current execution protocol;
[utils/README.md](utils/README.md) describes shared helpers.

The current final matrix has five families and ten cases (FP16 and FP8). Preserve existing
cases, pools, and recorded results unless the task explicitly changes them.
Softmax and the old vector families are retired. FP8 GEMM uses the current
family structure and authoritative FP8 example.
Shared-runner defaults come from the same family-owned case definitions.
Historical alignment reports describe earlier checkpoints; use current source
and family READMEs for the active contracts.

## Family layout

```text
experiments/<family>/
├── README.md                 Example source, shapes, pool, support, commands
├── cases.py                  Development/final workloads and model splits
├── spaces.py                 One expanded configuration pool
├── kernel.py                 Adapter to the authoritative example builder
├── reference.py              Independent mathematical reference
├── census.py                 Thin shared-census entry point
├── system/run.py             Thin shared-system entry point
├── tiletune/run.py            Thin shared-comparison entry point
└── heuristics/<GPU model>/    Audited best-config JSONs and oracle references
```

Keep experiment orchestration in `common/`, accessory functions in `utils/`, and
learned-model code in `xgboost/`. Reuse `comparison_main` and `census_main` from
`common/family_cli.py`, and `main(family=...)` from `common/system.py`.
Do not copy runners, GPU monitoring, cache handling, or result comparison into
family folders. Do not reintroduce `portable.py`, `legacy.py`, `kernels/tiled.py`,
alternative local kernels, or compatibility wrappers for removed paths.

## Reuse the example kernel

1. Identify the authoritative high-performance implementation under `examples/`
   and document the exact file and builder. Call that builder directly; do not
   write an approximate replacement in `experiments/`.
2. Implement `make_case(workload)` returning `utils.kernel.KernelCase` with
   `build`, seeded `inputs`, `reference`, `out_idx`, and relevant `pass_configs`.
   `build(**config)` must return the example's program through its public builder,
   `get_tir`, or `jit_impl.get_tir`, as appropriate.
3. Preserve layouts, dtype, accumulation, masks, causal bounds, shared-memory
   output paths, launch attributes, and compiler options. Honor output attributes
   already embedded by eager builders; do not supply a conflicting `out_idx`.
4. If the example needs an import guard, exposed launch parameter, or correctness
   fix, make the minimal change in the example itself so both entry points use it.
   Lock mutable eager-builder elaboration where needed, as attention does, without
   serializing the entire compile/benchmark pipeline.
5. Check outputs against the independent reference using `KernelCase.check`.
   Keep both elementwise and relative-norm checks. Do not relax tolerances to hide
   failures or compare a candidate against itself.

For orientation, GEMM uses `example_gemm_advanced_autotune.py` with A=(M,K),
B=(N,K), `transpose_B=True`, and the shared-memory epilogue. Attention uses
`example_mha_fwd_bshd.py`. KDA uses `examples/kda/chunk_o.py`; FP8 GEMM uses
`examples/gemm_fp8/example_tilelang_gemm_fp8.py`. These details are part of the kernel
contract, not tuning-method choices.

## Define cases and the single expanded pool first

- `cases(holdout=False)` supplies development workloads; `cases(holdout=True)`
  supplies final workloads with stable logical names. Existing core families have
  two cases in each split. Record shape, layout, and semantic options in
  `Workload.parameters`, and dtype in `Workload.dtype`; do not encode alternative
  implementations in the config grid.
- `training_cases()` supplies two training workloads and one validation workload
  for XGBoost. Their mathematical shapes must be disjoint from each other and the
  test workloads. Renaming the same workload does not make a separate split.
- `spaces.get_configs()` returns one deterministic, complete `expanded` pool
  using the example's native parameter names. Preserve every original example
  config, explicit default, and known best config.
- Aim for roughly eight times the example's autotuning grid by slicing existing
  ranges more finely or exposing meaningful example parameters. Every parameter
  must affect the example program. When the example has no meaningful autotuning
  grid, document the absolute expansion instead of claiming an artificial 8x.
- Keep one target-independent declared domain and stable ordering/config IDs.
  Do not maintain parallel small/large/exhaustive pools, cap final pools, silently
  map ignored knobs, or prune candidates with speculative legality filters.
  Compilation, correctness, and runtime failures remain recorded outcomes.
  Enforce mathematical support boundaries separately from candidate failures.
- Follow the existing `canonical_config` and `legality_reason` interfaces.
  Smoke/development and explicit config requests select original pool indices;
  they do not define a new pool. All tuning methods use the same ordered pool.

Current counts per case are GEMM 2,304 (288 × 8), attention 320, KDA 720 (90 × 8),
FP8 GEMM 2,304 (288 × 8), and grouped GEMM 192. Counts describe declared candidates, not guaranteed compilable
or distinct generated kernels. An intentional pool change needs new identities
and results; review `common/spaces.py::SPACE_VERSION` and frozen manifests.

## Register the family and its source identity

Check all integration points; adding a folder alone is insufficient:

| Location | Required integration |
| --- | --- |
| `families.py::FAMILIES` | Map operation name to family folder; keep imports lazy. |
| `common/spec.py` | Parameter schema, dtype/semantic validation, support checks, and family-owned default workloads. |
| `suite.py::CORE_OPS` and `common/system.py::OPS` | Register named-suite and system commands. Core membership changes the default matrix. |
| `utils/cli.py::SOURCE_ROOTS` | Include family and authoritative example sources in run provenance; exclude generated results. |
| `utils/baseline_store.py::EXAMPLES` / `measurement_sources` | Record the actual example and its dependencies as baseline provenance. |
| `xgboost/data.py::make_context` | Include the authoritative example in learned-model source identity too. |
| `manifests/`, `common/acceptance.py`, relevant tests | Keep frozen workloads and acceptance scope consistent with the intended matrix. |

`common/acceptance.py` currently recognizes ten cases across five operations
as the complete family matrix. If the task expands that matrix, update this
assumption, manifests, tests, and documentation together. Do not silently relabel
an expanded or partial study as the old complete final study.

Planning (`--plan`) must remain standard-library-only: no Torch, TileLang or
XGBoost import, hardware query, compilation, or result-directory creation.
Update the family README and relevant alignment documentation when contracts
change; retain the meaning of historical measurements.

## System and TileTune protocols

System tests use identical examples, cases, pools, inputs, and correctness checks
for `baseline`, `pipeline`, `grouped`, `multi_gpu`, and `combined`. The individual
variants enable only their named optimization; combined enables all three.
Use the shared runner's fresh processes and cold caches. Multi-GPU variants need
at least two idle devices of the same model. With only one available, report
those variants as unverified rather than claiming a complete ablation.

TileTune studies compare brute force, Carver, XGBoost, and TileTune. Collect the
three baselines explicitly with the family command's `--run-baselines` option,
using `common/study.py` and `utils/baseline_store.py`. Store them under
`<family>/results/<GPU model>/baselines/`; `current.json` identifies the current
complete bundle. Ordinary TileTune runs must only read saved baselines, including
when their identity is incompatible: request an explicit baseline rerun instead
of collecting automatically. Only an explicit baseline invocation may update the
current reference, and only after success. Preserve previous bundles referenced
by earlier studies. Use a new run output directory for each revision. Verify completion
manifests and artifact hashes; incomplete collections cannot become baselines.

Reuse saved baselines for the same workload, dtype, GPU and configuration pool.
Source/compiler/runtime changes, timing settings, baseline settings, TileTune
ranking changes, repeat seeds and evaluation K do not force recollection. Keep
their original provenance and costs; only an explicit `--run-baselines` invocation
refreshes them. A missing or different requested case/pool requires explicit
collection, never an automatic baseline run.
Follow the existing fixed baseline seed and XGBoost training/validation protocol;
do not train on final oracle labels or resample baselines for each TileTune repeat.

The reusable collection workflow currently runs on local CUDA. Reuse existing
Carver templates where available. Grouped GEMM and chunk KDA have explicit
mathematical templates; FP8 reuses MatmulTemplate. Preserve Carver's policy
scores and feasibility limits, recording any template approximations. An entirely
rejected pool records `model_unavailable` with a complete rejection report and
no fallback. Blackwell remains unsupported by the existing policy. Do not present
a new template as a precise instruction-timing model. See model_contracts.md.

Ampere/Hopper/Blackwell share the same example builders and ordered pools.
Keep system planning independent of a CUDA architecture. Let the compiler choose
the matrix/copy lowering: smoke evidence must inspect emitted instructions rather
than demand WGMMA, TCGEN05 or TMA merely because the device supports them. Include
grouped GEMM in matrix-instruction checks. Bind local study targets independently
with `utils.monitor.cuda_device`, honoring the caller's visible GPUs and restoring
visibility for the next target. Cross-compilation proves compilation only;
correctness and performance require the corresponding hardware.

## GPU monitoring and recorded results

Use idle GPUs of the intended model. System and sharded brute-force runners can
use all idle visible devices; the local study collector runs monitored workers
on a selected device. Do not promise automatic multi-GPU collection where the
runner does not implement it. Use the existing monitoring utilities throughout
measurement, not just a one-time idle check.

Workers record GPU/process observations every second. A foreign compute process
rejects the invocation; retain its logs separately and retry on idle hardware.
The sharded oracle runner retries contaminated shards automatically. Stop only
the experiment's own workers, never unrelated GPU jobs. Describe accepted runs
as observed uncontended: polling cannot exclude subsecond interference.

Save all per-config outcomes, indices/IDs, timings, correctness failures, source
and measurement identities, wall time, device metadata, and monitor evidence.
Use `common/brute_force.py` to export the existing GPU-specific heuristic schema
under `<family>/heuristics/<GPU model>/<workload>.json`, including the full oracle
path/hash and repeated winner measurements. Inspect the current GEMM H200 files
for the schema. Preserve historical artifacts; changed experiments need distinct
result locations. A winner-only JSON cannot replace a full oracle table.

Use `compare_results.py` (or `compare_results.sh`) to compare saved rankings or
selections by exact config identity at requested K values:

```text
Oracle@K = best valid latency in the full oracle
           / best valid oracle latency among the first K candidates
```

Higher is better; 1.0 matches the oracle. Failed candidates consume slots without
refilling K; no valid candidate is N/A; fewer than K candidates is a shortfall.
Keep ranking curves separate from actual saved online selections and tuning cost.
Read every comparison timing from the saved oracle for that case and GPU.
Record provenance differences without rejecting reuse or rerunning GPUs for an
offline comparison.

## Validation and commands

Run from the repository root. Replace `gemm`, workload names, device, paths, and
config indices with those for the new family after registration.

```bash
python -m experiments.gemm.system.run --plan
python -m experiments.gemm.tiletune.run --suite full --device hopper --plan

# Let the monitored runner select GPUs; the wrapper must not restrict it to one.
.agents/skills/tl-conda-gpu-run/scripts/run_in_tl.sh --no-gpu -- \
  python -m experiments.gemm.system.run --variant all \
  --config-indices 1534 1535 --output experiments/gemm/results/system-check-v1

.agents/skills/tl-conda-gpu-run/scripts/run_in_tl.sh --no-gpu -- \
  python -m experiments.gemm.tiletune.run --suite full --device hopper \
  --run-baselines

.agents/skills/tl-conda-gpu-run/scripts/run_in_tl.sh --no-gpu -- \
  python -m experiments.gemm.tiletune.run --suite full --device hopper \
  --output experiments/gemm/results/tiletune/revision-a

python -m experiments.compare_results --oracle PATH_TO_ORACLE_JSON \
  --tiletune PATH_TO_TILETUNE_JSON --carver PATH_TO_CARVER_JSON \
  --xgboost PATH_TO_XGBOOST_JSON --top-k 1 5 10 20 50
```

`full` executes complete final pools without asserting final acceptance. `final`
requires the passing development report and K=20. Begin with representative
config indices before launching a complete sweep; never report a subset check
as an exhaustive result. Use the repository GPU-run/build skill instructions
when executing tests or rebuilding native changes.

Before reporting a new family ready:

1. Verify example/adapter structural equality, independent numerical correctness,
   and relevant boundary shapes. Use `testing/python/experiments/test_example_kernels.py`
   as the pattern; include output handling under grouped compilation.
2. Verify deterministic pool order/IDs, example-config coverage, explicit subsets,
   disjoint model splits, and frozen-manifest consistency. Follow
   `test_example_pools.py`, `test_family_layout.py`, and `test_reusable_workflow.py`.
3. Exercise planning without GPU/compiler dependencies and run affected offline
   tests. Then validate representative configs through the actual monitored
   runners, all available system variants, and baseline collection/reuse.
4. For requested full studies, complete the sweep, repeated winner checks, and
   saved-result comparison. Compare example performance under identical timing,
   layout, dtype, and hardware conditions; record the FLOP convention for TFLOPS.
5. Report commands, artifact paths, pool/outcome counts, timing, monitor findings,
   and unsupported or untested scope. Distinguish completed measurements from
   planned runs. Do not overwrite prior results to make a check pass.
